\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Mean and daily Water mass transformation over 2014. a) Mean water mass transformation over 2014 for the ice pack, MIZ, and leads in each region. Hovmoller diagram of the water mass transformation (Sv) for the b) ice pack, c) ice pack linear kinematic features and d) the ratio of water mass transformation between the leads and the ice pack region. Panels e), f), and g) follow the same structure as panels b), c) and d), but for the MIZ, MIZ leads, and ratio between them. h) Mean water mass formation in density bins of $0.5kg/m^3$ during 2014 for the same regions as in panel a). Shaded gray and white areas in panels a) and h) represent the water masses of the Arctic ocean \citep  {Pemberton_WMT_2015}: the mixed layer polar surface water ($PSW_{ML}$; $\rho < 24.36 kg/m^3$), the halocline polar surface water ($PSW_{H}$; $24.36 kg/m^3 < \rho < 27.41 kg/m^3$), the polar deep water ($PDW$; $27.41 kg/m^3 < \rho < 27.81 kg/m^3$), and the Atlantic water ($AW$; $27.81 kg/m^3 < \rho $).}}{9}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fig4}{{4}{9}{Mean and daily Water mass transformation over 2014. a) Mean water mass transformation over 2014 for the ice pack, MIZ, and leads in each region. Hovmoller diagram of the water mass transformation (Sv) for the b) ice pack, c) ice pack linear kinematic features and d) the ratio of water mass transformation between the leads and the ice pack region. Panels e), f), and g) follow the same structure as panels b), c) and d), but for the MIZ, MIZ leads, and ratio between them. h) Mean water mass formation in density bins of $0.5kg/m^3$ during 2014 for the same regions as in panel a). Shaded gray and white areas in panels a) and h) represent the water masses of the Arctic ocean \citep {Pemberton_WMT_2015}: the mixed layer polar surface water ($PSW_{ML}$; $\rho < 24.36 kg/m^3$), the halocline polar surface water ($PSW_{H}$; $24.36 kg/m^3 < \rho < 27.41 kg/m^3$), the polar deep water ($PDW$; $27.41 kg/m^3 < \rho < 27.81 kg/m^3$), and the Atlantic water ($AW$; $27.81 kg/m^3 < \rho $)}{figure.4}{}}
